  "review": {
    "explanations": [
      {
        "item": "bookings.club_id",
        "explanation": "Every value already matches a club, so a foreign key to clubs was added safely. Bookings cannot point at a club that does not exist."
      },
      {
        "item": "bookings.member_uid",
        "explanation": "The Firebase user id became a uuid column named member_id, built deterministically with uuid5 so RLS can compare it to auth.uid(). The Supabase auth user ids must be generated the same way, or the policies will not match."
      },
      {
        "item": "bookings.starts_at",
        "explanation": "Some values were Firestore timestamps and others were ISO strings. Parsing the strings unifies the column as a timestamp, but check that the time zone of the ISO strings was interpreted correctly."
      },
      {
        "item": "clubs.owner_uid",
        "explanation": "Same approach as the member ids: the Firebase owner id became owner_id, a uuid5 value, so ownership-based RLS can work. It depends on the same auth id mapping being used everywhere."
      },
      {
        "item": "clubs.settings",
        "explanation": "The keys vary between documents, so storing it as jsonb keeps all data without forcing a rigid column list. Individual settings cannot be constrained or indexed by column this way."
      },
      {
        "item": "courts.hourly_rate",
        "explanation": "Most values were numbers and one numeric string was converted, so no data is lost. Still, confirm that the rate is in the currency and unit expected."
      },
      {
        "item": "members.dupr_rating",
        "explanation": "Two numeric strings were converted, but one non-numeric value became NULL. That value is lost from the table, so review the original document if it matters."
      },
      {
        "item": "members.uid",
        "explanation": "The Firebase user id became user_id, a uuid5 value, to support RLS checks against auth.uid(). Like the other ids, it only works if the auth accounts are created with matching ids."
      },
      {
        "item": "leagues.club_id",
        "explanation": "Every value matches a club, so the foreign key is safe and prevents orphaned leagues."
      },
      {
        "item": "payments.club_id",
        "explanation": "Every value matches a club, so the foreign key is safe and keeps payments tied to real clubs."
      },
      {
        "item": "payments.member_uid",
        "explanation": "The Firebase user id became member_id, a uuid5 value, to allow RLS to compare it to auth.uid(). It needs the same auth id mapping as the other member references."
      },
      {
        "item": "payments.metadata",
        "explanation": "The keys differ across documents, so jsonb preserves everything. Any metadata key you need to query often should be promoted to its own column later."
      },
      {
        "item": "payments.method",
        "explanation": "The map keys last4 and type appear in at least 90% of documents, so they became columns. Documents without them will have NULLs, and any other keys in the remaining documents need a check so nothing is silently dropped."
      },
      {
        "item": "bookings.players",
        "explanation": "Arrays of references were moved into the join table bookings_players, which is the normal relational form. Each player in a booking becomes one row, and it can be queried and constrained."
      },
      {
        "item": "members.favorite_courts",
        "explanation": "The array of court references moved to the join table members_favorite_courts, one row per member and court pair. This allows foreign keys to courts and makes the relation easy to query."
      }
    ],
